# `phpstan.neon` at `max` — for `lavaphp/core` alone.
#
# Two levels, one repository, and that is a deliberate split rather than an
# unfinished job. `phpstan.neon` holds the level everything must clear (8, all
# five packs plus the canonical app and its tests). This file holds the level
# the CORE must clear, and the difference is what the two trees are:
#
#   - core is the code an app author cannot replace. Every inference core makes
#     about a value it did not construct is a promise the framework keeps on
#     that author's behalf, so core is held to the strictest reading of its own
#     types — including the strict-array rules `max` adds, which is where the
#     remaining `offsetAccess` errors in the packs live.
#   - a pack is replaceable and an app is the user's own. Holding them at 8
#     keeps a real bar under them (the level that catches wrong argument types,
#     missing returns, and dead code) without making a pack author clear core's
#     bar to ship.
#
# Standalone rather than `includes: phpstan.neon`, so what it checks is exactly
# what this file says. NEON merges array parameters across includes, so an
# `includes` here would silently keep every path from the parent and the run
# would grow to the whole repository the moment someone added one there.
parameters:
    level: max
    paths:
        - packages/core/src
